WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.320
We all like to think we are unique.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.640
So they might be your DNA,
your fingerprints,

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.040
iris scans or retinal patterns.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.880
We are always leaving digital traces
wherever we go,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.800
and we can never be truly offline.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.960
There's always the suggestion that
collecting vast amounts of biometrics

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.920
is going to be used
for something nefarious.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
But it can actually create
some incredibly positive situations.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.920
So there's a saying in Chinese -

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.760
if you haven't done anything wrong,
why would you be scared of ghosts?

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.160
Businesses are typically designed
to make money

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.720
and they will not have,
in general,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.440
as much interest
in protecting your rights.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.720
You are very much the product.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.840
Fingerprints are something
that were used

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.360
in very, very early
Chinese pottery, for example.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.560
Where the potter would leave
their fingerprint in the wet clay.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.680
If you watch television and
you read all these forensic fiction,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.120
fingerprints solve everything
and the honest truth is they don't.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.560
The fingerprint in isolation
has no value

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.720
in telling you who the person is

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.480
until you have something
to compare it with.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.560
DNA is still the gold standard

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.160
when we can identify the source
of the DNA, whose DNA it is.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.000
What we're not very good
at understanding

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.120
is how did it get there?

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.120
So how did your DNA
get on to the knife?

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.440
Is it because you used the knife
to stab somebody?

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.880
Or is it because
you shook hands with somebody

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.840
who used the knife
to stab somebody?

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.080
If you look at
the little skin creases

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.040
that you have over your knuckles,

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.000
they're different on every finger,

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.160
they're different
across your two hands,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.160
they're different on identical twins.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.440
And when infrared light
shines on your veins

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.280
it interacts with
the deoxygenated blood

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.040
and your veins stand out
like black tramlines.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:23.040
There is a really strong chance
that the hand

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.760
and of all the biometrics
that we can see in the hand

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:30.200
may well be that 'holy grail'
that we search for

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.200
which is, it's unique.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.640
So there is a global trend
across the world

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.440
where computers collect more data.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.480
And therefore
governments and corporations

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.920
are locked in a race
where they're trying to collect

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.520
a mass amount
of identifying information.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:56.720
Gathering data about people who live
in a city or who live in a borough

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.600
is now a critical part
of the functioning government.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.480
If you want to
allocate your resources properly

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:03.680
you need the data.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:20.920
Wouldn't it just be wonderful

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.000
if you could put your hand or your
finger on whatever devices,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.800
it knows exactly who you are,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.840
it knows exactly
what your health statistics are,

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.440
what the probability is
of you catching cancer

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:33.960
because of the gene
that you've got -

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.200
the options are just limitless.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.760
Netflix released
what they thought was

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.200
anonymised records
of movie watching,

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.680
and their aim was to try and improve
their recommendation algorithms.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.840
In other words, if you've watched
movies A, B and C

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.440
you're really quite likely
to enjoy watching movie D.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.360
Unfortunately researchers
quickly realised

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:20.200
they were able to cross-reference
these anonymous records with IMDB,

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.240
which is an online
movie rating service.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.520
And once you can identify
who someone is

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:27.280
you can work out
their sexual preferences

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.120
to some degree of probability.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.440
It would be taking in all sorts
of data from local government,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.160
from your schools,
from your workplaces,

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:58.440
from your companies,
where you go, your GPS data -

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.560
putting it into one single score

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:04.680
which evaluates how trustworthy -
in quotation marks - you are.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:05.840
Like an Uber score

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.800
where, with a lower credit score,

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.680
you might not be able
to borrow money,

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.440
you might not be able to travel
on public transport.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.080
We're always
in a cyber-physical space.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.880
Every place we go to will have
some cyber element around it.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.480
There'll be cameras
capturing information.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.880
There'll be things
that are connecting to your phone

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:34.640
that you may or may not
be aware of.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.520
We are always leaving digital traces
wherever we go

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.640
and we can never be truly offline.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:43.440
In Shenzhen, when you're
crossing the road,

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.000
there are CCTV cameras
that use facial recognition

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.880
to see if you're jaywalking or not

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.880
and then identifying
who that citizen is

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.280
and then sometimes you'll be
displayed on a big billboard

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.640
on the other side of the road
in a sort of 'name and shame' action.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.240
What happens in China
and in other countries

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.720
is a cautionary lesson for all of us

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.120
that unless you have
proper regulation

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.800
the gathering of your data
is a real danger to our human rights.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.440
The landscape is
continually changing.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.240
Who would have thought ten years ago

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.800
we'd have had all this collection
that we have now?

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.160
Who would have thought about

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.280
the ways data can be used
and abused.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:43.920
So regulation is always
chasing where we are

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.680
with the current technology.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:55.120
If they provide you with pages
and pages of terms and conditions

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.040
well, that's transparent,
so we can all read those.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:01.600
However do we really have the time
and the knowledge

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.320
to read all of those?

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.440
No.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:08.520
So are we really in a situation
where it's not transparent

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.200
but it's apparent to us?

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:11.280
Are we aware?

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.720
Biometric data is very personal.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.680
It's your data
because it's about you.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.920
They're your fingerprints
or it's your DNA.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:21.560
Once it's given away
it is incredibly hard,

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.640
if not impossible,
to take it back.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.280
I have a real conflict

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:36.520
between how much of my information
I want to share

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:40.280
and how much information
I want to have available to me

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:41.720
when I go to a crime scene.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.320
I want to be able to find
the pieces of DNA,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.440
I want to be able
to find the fingerprints.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:50.280
I want to be able to get every single
bit of information I can

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:52.120
to make sure justice is served.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:56.160
But of course here I am, sitting
in the middle of that crime scene,

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:57.760
not wanting to give mine.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.280
That old saying,

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:01.520
if you have nothing to hide
you have nothing to fear -

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.880
that kind of approach
doesn't value our privacy.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.720
If the government said,

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.240
"We're going to put a camera in
every single bedroom in the country

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:13.000
that would film what happens
in your bedroom,

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.640
but we assure you we will only turn
it on if we think there's suspicion."

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.720
I think most members of the public
would say,

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:23.840
"I don't have anything to hide

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:26.360
but this is my bedroom,
this is private."

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:30.320
In reality, there are so many ways
to gather data about you nowadays

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.680
and it can give as detailed
information, in some ways, about you

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:35.920
as if you were having a camera
in your bedroom.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:45.880
Large size datasets or databases
are incredibly useful

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.440
but the critical thing
is that they are correctly used.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.720
That's going to be a very, very
big question that we need to resolve

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:56.080
as this digital word really
starts to accelerate around us.

